From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d3d9, d3d11: Share textures within the process without external memory

A Direct3D 9Ex device that decodes a movie into a shared texture which the same game's Direct3D 11 device opens (Koei Tecmo's
movie players) never showed it where the driver has no VK_KHR_external_memory_win32 (the console's): the D3D9 texture
could not be exported, its "shared handle" was INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE, and OpenSharedResource answered E_INVALIDARG. The probe
tests/d3d9ex-d3d11-share-probe.c measured it on the Pro: every step passes up to the open, which returns 80070057.

Both devices are in one process, so they can share the image itself once they share a Vulkan device:

- d3d11.dll lists its live devices (d3d11_local_share.cpp) and exports DXVKLocalShareGetDevice, which d3d9.dll finds with
  GetProcAddress if d3d11.dll is loaded. A D3D9Ex device made while a Direct3D 11 device lives, on the same GPU, runs on
  that device's DxvkDevice (D3D9FindD3D11Device), when the driver cannot share memory by handle, or when
  d3d9.shareD3D11DeviceAlways asks. d3d9.shareD3D11Device = False turns it off. d3d11.dll and d3d9.dll are pinned in
  memory once they take part, since each one's objects live on in the other's.
- A texture D3D9 is asked to share is made as a shared image is (one layout, deferred clears flushed), with no external
  memory, and listed in the device's table (DxvkLocalShares, dxvk_local_share.h) under a handle of its own, in a range no
  Windows handle of the process is in. A render target is also readable by shaders, as the D3D11 texture made from its
  description needs. The table is header-only and lives in the DxvkDevice, so both DLLs read the same one.
- OpenSharedResource looks the handle up in the table first: a D3D11 texture is made from the entry's description around the
  listed image (D3D11CommonTexture takes the image instead of creating one), after a check that the image is what the
  description would make, which names each property that is not. Textures opened keep the image alive after the D3D9
  texture is released.
- A clear of a texture that is listed is put in the commands that end each list, and a memory barrier ends them, since the
  other API's device reads the image next on the same queue: DXVK defers a clear to the next render pass or draw, which for a
  texture shared with another device would never come (the first Pro run opened the texture and read zeros).

Where the driver can share memory by handle nothing changes. Not covered: a Direct3D 9Ex device made before the Direct3D 11
one (the games measured make Direct3D 11 first; the other order stays as it was), and a Direct3D 11 texture opened by a
Direct3D 9 device.

The problem was found by reading bornaradusin/PC5 (LGPL-2.1-or-later) patches/dxvk 0001 to 0003 and its description of the Koei
Tecmo case; the design here is independent, with its own table in the device (zlib, as DXVK; NOTICE.md, "PC5").
---
diff --git a/dxvk.conf b/dxvk.conf
index 1c58703..fa7b994 100644
--- a/dxvk.conf
+++ b/dxvk.conf
@@ -598,6 +598,28 @@
 # d3d9.dpiAware = True
 
 
+# Share textures with a Direct3D 11 device of the process
+#
+# A Direct3D 9Ex device made while a Direct3D 11 device of the process lives runs on that device's
+# Vulkan device, so a texture it shares (a movie player's frames, say) can be opened by the Direct3D 11
+# device without Win32 external memory, which some drivers (a console's) do not have. Where the driver
+# can share memory through Win32 handles, handles are used as before. False turns this off.
+#
+# Supported values: True, False
+
+# d3d9.shareD3D11Device = True
+
+
+# Share textures with a Direct3D 11 device even where handles work
+#
+# Also keep shared textures inside the process where the driver could share them through Win32
+# handles, to try the path on such a driver. Only has an effect with d3d9.shareD3D11Device.
+#
+# Supported values: True, False
+
+# d3d9.shareD3D11DeviceAlways = False
+
+
 # Lenient Clear
 #
 # Decides whether or not we fastpath clear anyway if we are close enough to
